{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfeL5VWbJXhEhWTqpBT2Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 01 is done and the gate passes. Over everything I could read, I found no earlier report that Chu & Stuckey's definite move or better move is wrong, and nobody has published a correction.\n\n**What was swept.** OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar and OpenCitations list 144 distinct works that cite either the CP 2009 paper, Chu's 2011 thesis, or the thesis's IJCAI 2013 abstract.\n- **CP paper:** 38 real citers. 24 of them (63%) were read in full; for the rest no full text could be found, open or behind a paywall.\n- **Thesis or abstract only:** 103 citers, 32 of them (31%) read in full. Almost all cite it for the Chuffed solver or lazy clause generation, not for the open-stacks rules.\n\nEach full text was searched for the rules and the theorem numbers. Every passage that mentions open stacks or cites the paper was then read in context.\n\n**What turned up:**\n- **One more restatement.** Fink (2012), a PhD thesis at ICMC-USP, restates Theorem 1 (the definite move) on pp. 27–28, with the published premise, as true. Our counterexample graph refutes it too. I saved it as `literature/fink_2012_phd_thesis_mosp_novas_contribuicoes.pdf`. Nobody restates the better move.\n- **Three works use an implementation of the rules, and none reports a failure.**\n  - Chu, Garcia de la Banda & Stuckey (2010, 2012) build them into a constraint-programming model as dominance-breaking constraints.\n  - Frinhani et al. (2018) ran the original C code to get their published optima, and Martin, Yanasse & Pinto (2022) reuse those values.\n- **A related observation, not about the premise.** Beck, Kuroiwa, Lee, Stuckey & Zhong (CP 2025, Prop. 3 and Example 6) warn that two moves which dominate each other can make a satisfiable instance look unsatisfiable. That is the general form of our earlier `better_move` cycle bug.\n\n**Unread.** 14 citers of the CP paper had no copy I could get, plus Gange, Chu & Stuckey (2019), whose author page blocked the fetch. They are listed in `literature/MISSING.md`. The one to fetch first is De Giovanni et al. (2013, *ITOR*), because it is an exact method for gate matrix connection cost and the most likely to reuse the rules. Two other unread entries look like false index links: both are *ITOR* papers on efficiency analysis.\n\n**Where it is:**\n- The table, one row per citer, and the closing verdict are in `paper2/prior_art_counterexample.md`, under \"Every citer, swept\". I also added a one-line pointer at the top of that file.\n- `python3 -m paper2.prior_art_sweep` regenerates `paper2/data/prior_art/citers.csv` and `hits.txt`.\n- Downloaded PDFs are in `paper2/data/prior_art/fulltext/`, which is git-ignored (about 55 MB). Every URL fetched by hand is recorded in the script.\n\n**Checks.** The gate passed: Lean builds with its one known `sorry`, 1,361 MOSP tests and 114 pathwidth_solver tests pass. The split run's log has no `!!!` line. No published claim of ours needed fixing. PROGRESS.md is updated to 1/9 and the item is marked `[x]`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":419,"cache_read_input_tokens":256851,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":419,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":775,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3780c788-a5fb-42f1-accc-acf3dc2fde45","uuid":"71fbb1e2-f52d-4155-a65f-91807b3c657d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T00:07:46.264Z","request_id":"req_011CfeL5V3owrFC4uSZTuWcU"}
